## What `crimes context src/Card.tsx` adds over the scan

```
risk: MEDIUM  (3 findings: 0 high, 1 medium, 2 low)
Clues
  · churn: 2 commits over 90d · last touched 2 months ago · 1 author
  · test gap: top-quartile
Likely tests
  (no sibling, __tests__, .test, .spec, _test, or _spec files matched the target basename)
```

Two things that aren't in the scan JSON's finding list: the file has **no tests at all** (which is why `test_gap: 1.0` on all three findings), and **0 importers** — so blast radius is zero and every fix here is contained. That combination is the good case for a refactor: nothing downstream breaks, but nothing catches a regression either, so the change needs a test added in the same commit.

The file itself (`src/Card.tsx:8-32`) is 25 lines, all three crimes in one JSX tree.

---

## The three fixes

### 1. `accessible_interaction_risk` — medium, `crime_00001`

> `line 10: <div> with onClick has no role / aria-label / tabIndex`

This is the only medium and the only one that's a real user-facing defect rather than a maintenance hazard. Keyboard users can't focus the div, screen readers don't announce it as actionable, and there's no Enter/Space handler.

The detector's `fix_shape` is *"use semantic elements; add ARIA only when semantics don't fit"* — so `role="button" tabIndex={0}` is the fallback, not the fix. The fix is a real `<button>`.

**But there's a decision to make first**, because `Card` takes arbitrary `children`. If those children ever contain a link or button, wrapping the whole card in `<button>` nests interactive content — invalid HTML, and screen readers flatten it. Two shapes:

- **A. Whole card is the target, children are inert** → outer element becomes `<button type="button">`. Simplest.
- **B. Children may be interactive** → card stays a `<div>`, and a real `<button>` inside gets stretched over the card with `::after`. Preserves a single tab stop and correct semantics.

I'd go with **A** for this component and make the constraint explicit in the type, since nothing imports it yet and there's no evidence of interactive children:

```tsx
export function Card({
  children,
  onActivate,
  label,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onActivate?: () => void;
  label?: string;
}) {
  const Root = onActivate ? "button" : "div";
  return (
    <Root
      type={onActivate ? "button" : undefined}
      onClick={onActivate}
      aria-label={onActivate ? label : undefined}
      className="…"
    >
```

Making `onActivate` optional also fixes something the detector can't see: the current `onClick={() => console.log("clicked")}` is a placeholder. A non-interactive card shouldn't render as a button at all, so the accessibility affordance follows the prop rather than being unconditional.

### 2. `design_token_escape` — low, `crime_00002`

> `9 hard-coded style values across 4 kinds (color, px, rgb, shadow)`
> `line 10: color #ff00aa` · `#0a0a0a` · `#cccccc` · `#1a73e8` · `rgb rgb(0, 0, 0)`

The summary names the actual risk: *"agents extending the file may copy them forward without realising they bypass the tokens."* That's the agent-risk framing, and it's right — the next agent to add a variant to this card will copy `#cccccc` rather than look up the border token.

The important finding here isn't in the JSON: **`tailwind.config.ts` only defines three tokens** (`brand: #0050ff`, `surface: #f7f7fa`, `ink: #111111`), and **none of the six colours in `Card.tsx` map onto them**. So this isn't "swap literal for token" — the token scale is genuinely missing the roles this component needs. Fixing the file without extending the config would just push the literals into `arbitrary-[#cccccc]` classes, which the detector would rightly still flag.

Extend the config first:

```ts
export default {
  theme: {
    colors: {
      brand: "#0050ff",
      surface: "#f7f7fa",
      ink: "#111111",
      "ink-muted": "#333333",
      "ink-subtle": "#666666",
      border: "#cccccc",
    },
    boxShadow: { card: "0 2px 4px rgb(0 0 0 / 0.2)" },
  },
};
```

Then the per-value mapping, with two that need a human call rather than a mechanical swap:

| Literal | Token | Note |
|---|---|---|
| `background: "#ff00aa"` | `bg-surface` | **Needs your call.** Magenta matches no token and reads like fixture filler. If it's intentional brand accent, it needs a named token, not a swap. |
| `color: "#0a0a0a"` | `text-ink` | `#111111` — imperceptible shift |
| `borderColor: "#cccccc"` | `border-border` | new token |
| `outlineColor: "#1a73e8"` | `outline-brand` | **Behaviour change.** `#1a73e8` is Google blue, almost certainly copy-pasted; `brand` is `#0050ff`. Worth confirming, but a focus ring that isn't your brand colour is the bug. |
| `boxShadow: "0 2px 4px rgb(0,0,0)"` | `shadow-card` | also softens the fully-opaque black, which was almost certainly unintended |
| `#333333` / `#666666` (l.24, l.27) | `text-ink-muted` / `text-ink-subtle` | |

### 3. `responsive_fragility` — low, `crime_00003`

> `line 10: width 800px` · `line 24: width 720px` · `line 24: fontSize 18px (no @media in file)`
> `no @media query found in the file`

`width: 800` on the root is the actual breakage — it's a hard width, not a max, so the card overflows every phone viewport. Note `maxWidth: 1024` sits right beside it and is dead code: a fixed `width` makes `maxWidth` unreachable. The nested `width: 720` + `width: 240` are a hand-rolled two-column split that can't collapse.

The detector's `fix_shape` — *"drive layout from container queries or a fluid scale, not pixel media queries"* — is the better guidance here than the generic `suggested_action` (which says add `@media`). Since `Card` has zero importers, it doesn't know its own viewport context; container queries are the correct tool:

- Root: `w-full max-w-4xl` — `max-w-4xl` is 896px, so keep `maxWidth: 1024` as `max-w-[64rem]` if 1024 was deliberate. Either way `width` stops being fixed.
- Split: `@container` on the root, then `grid grid-cols-1 @2xl:grid-cols-[1fr_240px]` — collapses to stacked below the container breakpoint, no media query needed.
- `fontSize: 18` → `text-lg` (`1.125rem`), so it respects user font-size settings. `px` doesn't.

