## Resulting file

```tsx
export function Card({
  children,
  onActivate,
  label,
}: {
  children: React.ReactNode;
  onActivate?: () => void;
  label?: string;
}) {
  const Root = onActivate ? "button" : "div";
  return (
    <Root
      type={onActivate ? "button" : undefined}
      onClick={onActivate}
      aria-label={onActivate ? label : undefined}
      className="@container w-full max-w-4xl p-6 grid grid-cols-1 gap-6
                 @2xl:grid-cols-[1fr_240px]
                 bg-surface text-ink border border-border shadow-card
                 outline-brand focus-visible:outline-2"
    >
      <div className="text-lg text-ink-muted">{children}</div>
      <div className="text-sm text-ink-subtle">sidebar</div>
    </Root>
  );
}
```

Every `style={{}}` object is gone, which is what clears all three detectors at once — they all scan inline JSX style values. That's not a coincidence worth relying on, though: the token and responsive fixes are only real because the config gained the missing tokens and the layout gained a collapse rule.

## Before you land it

Three things I'd want settled, in order of how much they'd change the diff:

1. **`#ff00aa` and `#1a73e8`** — these are the only two swaps that are visible behaviour changes rather than refactors. The rest are within a shade of the existing tokens.
2. **Interactive children** — if `Card` will ever wrap a link, shape B (stretched link) instead of shape A.
3. **A test.** `test_gap` is `1.0` on all three findings and `crimes` found no test file. A `Card.test.tsx` asserting the root renders as `<button>` when `onActivate` is passed and `<div>` when it isn't would close the gap and lock in the accessibility fix — the one that can silently regress.

Then re-run the gate: `crimes scan src/Card.tsx --format json` should come back with `summary.total: 0`.

Want me to apply this — config, component, and test — or adjust the two colour calls first?
